{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Natural selection has no conscious goal; scientific method has the normative goal of truth/error elimination","Biological survival is an end in itself; scientific corroboration is a means to further testing","Evolution accumulates adaptations; Popperian science aims to discard errors, not accumulate confirmed truths"],"overturn_conditions":["If it can be shown that maximizing test severity logically requires assuming the uniformity of nature","If a formal definition of verisimilitude is recovered that avoids the Tichý/Miller critique and supports the preference","If 'acting on' a theory is proven to logically entail an inductive commitment that cannot be bracketed"],"shared_structure":["Selection of the survivor of the harshest environment/tests","Goal is not prediction of future survival but optimization of the selection process","No assumption that the survivor is 'true' or 'perfect', only 'best so far'"],"source_memory_refs":["evolutionary_selection_heuristic","debugging_strategy_worst_case"],"transfer_claims":["Just as we study the fittest organism to understand limits of adaptation, we use the best-corroborated theory to probe the limits of current knowledge","Preferring the fittest organism for study does not imply it will survive forever; similarly, preferring a theory for action does not imply it is reliable"]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories in action is defensible without smuggling induction by reframing 'preference' as a methodological rule to maximize the severity of future tests, not as an inductive inference of reliability; however, this defense costs the agent any rational assurance that acting on the theory will succeed, reducing practical action to a non-rational 'leap' consistent with his critical rationalism but unsatisfying for pragmatic decision-making.","counterconditions":["If the agent requires a probability > 0.5 that the